#Navigation

Command: man command
Function: Manual page for selected command
Example:
root@ubuntu:~$ man
What manual page do you want?
For example, try 'man man'.
root@ubuntu:~$ man ls

Command: pwd
Function: Print working directory
Example:
root@ubuntu:~$ pwd
/root

Command: ls
Function: List contents in current directory
Example:
root@ubuntu:~$ ls
filesystem

Command: ls -a
Function: List ALL contents in current directory including hidden files
Example:
root@ubuntu :~ $ ls -a
etc
home
lib
lib.usr-is-merged
1ib64
lost+found

Command: ls -l
Function: List contents in current directory including permissions, group and user of content.
Example:
root@ubuntu:~$ ls -l
-rw-r--r--1 root root 510 Oct 5 21:14 multiline.txt
drwxr-xr-x 2 root root 4096 Oct 5 21:20 my_directory_copy
drwxr-xr-x 2 root root 4096 Oct 5 21:30 project

Command: ls -R
Function: List recurring/ nested content in directories
Example:
root@ubuntu:~$ mkdir -p project/src/components
root@ubuntu:~$ ls -R project
project:
src
project/src:
components
project/src/components:

Command: lsof
Function: List of open files including process ID's and more.
Example:
admin@ip-10-1-13-82:~$ lsof /var/log/bad.log
COMMAND   PID USER  FD TYPE DEVICE SIZE/OFF NODE NAME
badlog-py 593 admin 3w REG 259,1 17445 265802 /var/log/bad.log

Command: cd
Function: To change to a specific directory
Example:
root@ubuntu:~$ cd filesystem
root@ubuntu:~/filesystem$ mkdir hello

Command: cd ..
Function: To return to previous directory
Example:
root@ubuntu:~/filesystem$ cd ..
root@ubuntu:~$

Command: cd /
Function: To enter root directory
Example:
root@ubuntu:/$ cd /
root@ubuntu:/$ pwd
/

Command: history
Function: To final all commands used in current terminal, use !NUMBER to use a previous command.
Example:
root@ubuntu:/$ history
1 history
2 ls
3 ls -l
4 cat filesystem
5 cd /home
6 cd ..
7 history
root@ubuntu:/$ !5
cd /home

Command: alias
Function: To create short cut commands.
Example:
root@ubuntu:~$ alias
alias egrep='egrep -- color=auto'
alias fgrep='fgrep -- color=auto'
alias grep='grep -- color=auto'
alias l='ls -CF'
alias la='ls -A'
alias ll='ls -alF'
alias ls='ls -- color=auto'
root@ubuntu:~$ l
filesystem@ greet.sh*
root@ubuntu:~$ alias hello='echo "Hello World"'
root@ubuntu:~$ hello
Hello World

Piping Commands: Piping in Linux connects the standard output (STDOUT) of one command directly into the standard input (STDIN) of another command using the vertical bar symbol.
Example:
root@ubuntu:~$ head -n 10 multiline.txt | tail -n 5
Line 6: This is the sixth line.
Line 7: This is the seventh line.
Line 8: This is the eighth line.
Line 9: This is the ninth line.
Line 10: This is the tenth line.
